course: dictionary
lesson: 10152
title: Throwaway: waste and words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Throwaway is a word you will meet in science, geography and English. Read the words around it to find its meaning, because they are its context and they leave clues.
explain: «The order is reduce first, reuse second and recycle third, because recycling still uses energy; a reusable bottle can replace hundreds of throwaway ones.» The clue is reusable. A throwaway bottle is the opposite: you use it once and then bin it.
explain: Throwaway can also describe words. A throwaway remark is said quickly and casually, without much thought, though it may still matter. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Throwaway means made to be used once or for a short time, then thrown away, as in throwaway cups.
fact: A throwaway remark is said quickly and casually, without much thought, but it can still be important.
fact: A throwaway society buys cheap things and throws them away too fast, which wastes resources and causes pollution.
fact: A throwaway (noun) is something thrown away, or something treated as unimportant.
fact: Reduce, reuse, recycle is the order to follow: reduce first, reuse second and recycle third, because recycling still uses energy.
exercise: «A reusable bottle can replace hundreds of throwaway ones.» What does throwaway mean here? | Kept for a long time and washed | Very expensive | Made of glass | *Used once and then thrown away
exercise: Mum says, «That was just a throwaway remark.» What does she mean? | It was written down on paper | It was a long, careful speech | *It was said quickly and casually, without much thought | It was the most important thing said all day
exercise: In «The order is reduce first, reuse second and recycle third», which step comes first? | Recycle | Throwaway | Reuse | *Reduce
test: A throwaway society buys cheap things and throws them away too fast. What problem does this cause? | More money saved for everyone | Less rubbish in the world | Stronger, longer-lasting goods | *Waste and pollution
test: «The old pens were treated as throwaways.» What does throwaways mean here? | Expensive gifts kept forever | Writing tools for art lessons | *Things treated as unimportant and thrown away | Things that are kept safe in a drawer
exam: Why is a reusable bottle better than throwaway bottles? | Because it is made to be used only once | Because it is cheaper to throw away | Because it uses no energy at all | *Because it can replace hundreds of throwaway bottles
